 W(2, DUP, '`emails = ["a@x.com", "b@x.com", "a@x.com", "c@x.com", "b@x.com"]`. Remove the duplicates but keep the first-seen order, using a loop and a new list. Print it.', 'emails = ["a@x.com", "b@x.com", "a@x.com", "c@x.com", "b@x.com"]\n', 'emails = ["a@x.com", "b@x.com", "a@x.com", "c@x.com", "b@x.com"]\nunique = []\nfor e in emails:\n    if e not in unique:\n        unique.append(e)\nprint(unique)', "Add an item only if it is `not in` the new list yet. Unlike `set()`, this keeps the original order."),
 F(2, LOOP, "This code should remove every even number and print `[5]`, but it prints `[4, 5]`. Fix it.", 'nums = [2, 4, 5, 6]\nfor n in nums:\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        nums.remove(n)\nprint(nums)', 'nums = [2, 4, 5, 6]\nfor n in nums[:]:\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        nums.remove(n)\nprint(nums)', "Removing items while looping over the same list makes the loop skip items. Loop over a copy, `nums[:]`, and remove from the original."),
 W(2, BUILT, '`marks = [45, 82, 67, 90, 38]`. Print the average rounded to 1 decimal place, and whether all marks are 35 or more (use `all()`), with one `print()`.', 'marks = [45, 82, 67, 90, 38]\n', 'marks = [45, 82, 67, 90, 38]\nprint(round(sum(marks) / len(marks), 1), all(m >= 35 for m in marks))', "Average = `sum()` ÷ `len()`. `all()` is `True` only if the check is `True` for every item."),
 # ---------- Challenge (same chapter, combined) ----------
 W(3, SORT, '`words = ["kiwi", "banana", "fig", "apple"]`. Print the words sorted by length, shortest first.', 'words = ["kiwi", "banana", "fig", "apple"]\n', 'words = ["kiwi", "banana", "fig", "apple"]\nprint(sorted(words, key=len))', "`key=len` tells `sorted()` to compare the lengths instead of the words themselves."),
 W(3, LOOP, '`readings = [12.5, -1, 13.2, -1, 12.9]`, where -1 means a missing reading. Build a clean list without the -1 values, then print the average of the clean readings rounded to 2 decimals.', 'readings = [12.5, -1, 13.2, -1, 12.9]\n', 'readings = [12.5, -1, 13.2, -1, 12.9]\nclean = []\nfor r in readings:\n    if r != -1:\n        clean.append(r)\nprint(round(sum(clean) / len(clean), 2))', "Clean first, then calculate: the average must divide by the number of real readings (3), not 5."),
 W(3, NEST, '`marks = [["Asha", 78], ["Ravi", 92], ["Neha", 85]]`. Use a loop to find and print the name with the highest mark.', 'marks = [["Asha", 78], ["Ravi", 92], ["Neha", 85]]\n', 'marks = [["Asha", 78], ["Ravi", 92], ["Neha", 85]]\nbest = marks[0]\nfor row in marks:\n    if row[1] > best[1]:\n        best = row\nprint(best[0])', "Each row is a small list: `row[0]` is the name and `row[1]` the mark. Keep the best row seen so far."),
 F(3, COPY, "This code should keep a backup of the old list and print `[10, 20, 30]`. Fix it.", 'original = [10, 20, 30]\nbackup = original\noriginal.append(40)\nprint(backup)', 'original = [10, 20, 30]\nbackup = original.copy()\noriginal.append(40)\nprint(backup)', "`backup = original` is not a copy: both names point to one list. `copy()` (or `[:]`) makes a real copy."),
 W(3, CHG, '`nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]`. Replace the middle two items (3 and 4) with 30 and 40 using ONE slice assignment. Then print the list reversed with a slice.', 'nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\n', 'nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\nnums[2:4] = [30, 40]\nprint(nums[::-1])', "A slice on the left of `=` replaces that part of the list. `[::-1]` reads the list backwards."),
 T(3, BUILT, 'nums = [4, 9, 1, 7]\nprint(sorted(nums)[-2:], max(nums) - min(nums), sum(nums) // len(nums))', "`sorted(nums)[-2:]` is the two biggest values. The range is 9 − 1 = 8, and 21 // 4 = 5."),
 W(3, REM, '`queue = ["T1", "T2", "T3", "T4"]`. Serve tickets from the front with `pop(0)` until only 2 are left, printing `Served T1` and so on. Then print the queue.', 'queue = ["T1", "T2", "T3", "T4"]\n', 'queue = ["T1", "T2", "T3", "T4"]\nwhile len(queue) > 2:\n    ticket = queue.pop(0)\n    print("Served", ticket)\nprint(queue)', "`pop(0)` removes the first item and gives it back, so you can print it. The loop stops when 2 are left."),
